__pycache__/
*.pyc
venv/
.pytest_cache/
# generated at runtime -- not source
#
# EVERY .db UNDER var/lib, not a list of the ones that existed when
# this was written. The list had already fallen behind twice:
# config_history.db was committed by a `git add -A`, and the dev-silo
# databases are BUILT by scripts/seed_dev_silos.py from the .sql files
# beside them -- which are the real source and stay tracked.
#
# A tracked database is worse than clutter. It changes on every reload,
# sync or login, so the tree is permanently dirty, and `git am` refuses
# to apply a patch over a modified file. That cost five rounds of
# debugging a patch that was correct the whole time.
deployment/var/log/
deployment/var/lib/**/*.db
# AND THE WHOLE MIRROR, which the rule above missed. The Iceberg
# warehouse is Parquet, Avro and JSON -- not a single .db -- so 29
# generated files were tracked, every sync rewrote them under new
# names, and the tree was permanently dirty in exactly the way the
# paragraph above warns about.
#
# It cost the thing that comment predicts. A patch could not be
# applied because a tracked manifest had changed since it was written,
# and three commits went missing while that was untangled.
#
# UNTRACKING IS A SEPARATE STEP, deliberately left to a person:
#
#     git rm -r --cached deployment/var/lib/mirror
#
# A patch cannot do it, because `git am` verifies the content of every
# file it deletes and a mirror changes on every sync -- so the patch
# that removes it can never apply to a machine that has synced since.
# That is how this rule came to be needed twice.
#
# The mirror is rebuilt by scripts/run_sync.py from the silos, which
# are the real source.
# a curl session jar, written while testing by hand
deployment/var/lib/mirror/
cookies.txt
# secrets, if you ever add a .env for a real API key
.env
